\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Easier amid doubts on US, yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,095 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2001\par 11:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 9 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were easier late on Monday amid doubts over the yen's firmer tone and on dampened hopes of a quick recovery in the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The yen briefly poked above 125.50 from Friday's lows beyond 126 on profit-taking in dollars, but most players remained bearish given Japan's grim economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  Weak U.S. jobs data on Friday weighed more on regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the dollar as it raised concerns about a much awaited revival in Asia's biggest export market. \par \par \uc2  The data showed 114,000 non-farm jobs lost in June, more than double the forecast, and the jobless rate rising to 4.5 percent from 4.4 percent in May. \par \par \uc2  Speculation some of Asia's central banks, pressured by poor economic numbers and the uncertain outlook, may allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to settle in weaker ranges also favoured the dollar. \par \par \uc2  No aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen when the Taiwan dollar hit fresh 34-month lows and the Singapore dollar headed back towards 11-year troughs near 1.8290. \par \par \uc2  Sharp losses in the region's major equity indices, tracking overnight falls in U.S. stock markets, also weighed. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar took a hit as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped back from its six-week defence of the 34.50 level, letting it reach lows of 34.65, last seen in early September 1998. \par \par \uc2  It was also weighed by losses in Taipei shares , which closed at fresh six-month lows as investors focused on Wall Street's performance instead of recent state moves to boost market liquidity. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the next big target for the Taiwan dollar was at 35.00, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was expected to slow the pace of its decline. \par \par \uc2  Technical signals also suggested the Taiwan dollar might be in for a breather as the U.S. dollar was back in overbought territory with a 14-day relative strength index reading of 77.9. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar lingered close to 11-year lows around 1.8290 hit late Friday ahead of Tuesday's second quarter preliminary gross domestic product estimates, which are expected to show continued weakness. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said it was also weighed by players betting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hint at a softening of its monetary bias in a policy review statement, due on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But most players were reluctant to take aggressive positions due to fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . With the market already long on U.S. dollars, players were also cautious of profit-taking if the scheduled events deliver what the market is expecting. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht bumped against 45.50 as its borrowing costs eased offshore, allowing players to beef up their dollar longs. \par \par \uc2  But wariness over \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  checked the baht's decline while local banks and corporates capped its upside at around 45.30. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Pridiyathorn Devakula confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the baht last week. Dealers suspected the BOT had sold at least $100 million to shore up the baht when it hit lows of 45.70 last Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Pridiyathorn said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quell volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market but would not resist the regional trend. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah firmed slightly on a lack of demand for the dollar from local corporates, some of whom were instead seen buying rupiah for tax payments. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers in Jakarta said its upside was capped with interbank players bidding for dollars around 11,320 levels. \par \par \uc2  The political outlook remained grim as President Abdurrahman Wahid gave his opponents a deadline of July 20 to back off from efforts to oust him or said he would declare a state of emergency. Impeachment hearings are set to begin on August 1. \par \par \uc2  Wahid says parliament's plans to consider impeaching him were illegal and a threat to the nation. \par \par \uc2  The announcement came after political leaders, including vice president Megawati Sukarnoputri, failed to attend a meeting called by Wahid to resolve the issue. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won revisited recent lows of 1,298 per dollar as Korean shares fell to 10-week troughs on worries about the prospects for vital tech exports, following a spate of earnings warnings by major U.S. and European tech firms. The expected settlement of share sales by foreign investors also weighed on the won. Foreign investors sold a net 181.2 billion won on the Korean stock market on Monday, after selling a net 103.8 billion won on Friday. \par \par \uc2  After a two-month uptrend dating back to April 4, the won has run into some resistance in the past month, failing to strengthen past the 1,290 level since June 12 as the yen weakened against the dollar. But hopes of foreign investment flows have kept it from weakening past 1,310. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso ended onshore trade slightly higher as corporates stayed out of the market and interbank players booked profit on dollar longs amid fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Manila said the speculation was sparked by governor Rafael Buenaventura's comment on Sunday that the peso should be "better than 53 this week". \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was also cautious after President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo said she would announce plans to fix economic problems this week. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar remained overbought with a 14-day RSI reading of 82.14. It has been above the overbought level of 70 since mid-June, reaching a peak of 95 when it hit 5-= month highs of 53.10 pesos last week, according to Reuters data. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  #N/A DRP GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     125.52       125.77         +0.20\par  Sing dlr      1.8280       1.8256         -0.13\par  Taiwan dlr    34.545       34.473         -0.21\par  Korean won   1296.20      1289.00         -0.56\par  Baht           45.41        45.40         -0.02\par  Peso           52.83        52.89         +0.11\par  Rupiah      11330.00     11335.00         +0.04\par  Ringgit       3.8000       3.7995         -0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     125.52       114.45         -8.82\par  Sing dlr      1.8280       1.7365         -5.01\par  Taiwan dlr    34.545       32.900         -4.76\par  Korean won   1296.20      1277.00         -1.48\par  Baht           45.41        43.70         -3.77\par  Peso           52.83        50.75         -3.94\par  Rupiah    