\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Up On Stronger Yen, Caution On Rate Review \b0\par\par\uc2 934 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2001\par 13:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht finished slightly higher against the U.S. dollar Thursday on a stronger yen and caution ahead of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  review of monetary policy intended to limit capital outflows, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 45.330 baht, down from THB45.375 at the Asian close Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to THB45.400 after Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said the country would maintain a low-interest rate policy, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But Thaksin didn't rule out the possibility that an upcoming review of monetary policy will result in a hike in interest rates, so some players unloaded dollars and dragged the pair off its high. \par \par \uc2  Most investors were sidelined pending a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting with onshore bank treasurers Friday to explain new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reporting requirements for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other transactions, to be implemented July 2. \par \par \uc2  The tightened reporting requirements are to improve the ability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to monitor capital flows and compliance with existing controls on baht outflows. \par \par \uc2  Incoming Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said he will conduct a review of monetary policy with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after his appointment is formally confirmed. The confirmation should take place late this week. \par \par \uc2  Pridiyathorn said he was considering changes to limit capital outflows, but any changes in interest rates, capital controls or other elements of monetary policy would be introduced as a package. \par \par \uc2  Pridiyathorn and government officials have said interest rates have stayed too low for too long, resulting in undesirable capital outflows. \par \par \uc2  Outgoing Governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul maintained that low interest rates were essential to support recovery in the economy and the financial sector, and that outflows were positive, as they were mostly to repay foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  A slightly worse-than-expected trade deficit in April of $134 million announced Thursday had little effect on the baht, as the market had known the figures would be disappointing. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the baht to open Friday in a range of THB45.350-THB45.500, subject to movements of other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar, with limited participation from offshore players ahead of the implementation of the new reporting requirements. \par \par \uc2  Yields on long-term Thai government bonds stabilized following 90-to-100 basis point increases in the previous two days. However, yields on selected short-term issues continued to rise, in spite of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying in three- and four-year bonds, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Late selling by mutual funds - to prepare cash for redemption by bond-fund unit holders - pushed up the yield on the illiquid government bond due October 2003 to around 4.00%, from a last deal Wednesday of 3.39%, dealers said. Yields move in the opposite direction to prices. \par \par \uc2  The yield on government bonds due December 2008 finished flat from the previous close, however, around 5.50%, off a high of 5.60%. \par \par \uc2  HSBC Treasurer Mark Boyne said Thursday he expects volatility in a range of about 20 basis points from current levels until the incoming \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor clarifies whether or not he intends to hike short-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand released Thursday a schedule for weekly Treasury bill auctions in June, beginning Monday, of very small THB1 billion tranches of both 28-day and 91-day T-bills. \par \par \uc2  The small offerings should enable the authorities to keep T-bill yields down despite the uncertainty, possibly by ordering state banks to bid near the average yields accepted at this week's auctions, around 1.61% for the 28-day and 1.735% for the 91-day T-bills. \par \par \uc2  The June schedule for Tuesday auctions of Financial Institution Development Fund, or FIDF, bonds was also released Thursday. The first auction on June 5 will be of THB2 billion in two-year bonds and THB3 billion in five-year bonds. \par \par \uc2  Small closing deals in certain FIDF bonds recorded Wednesday at sub-market level yields indicated that state agencies might also be ordered to submit low-yield bids at auctions. \par \par \uc2  A schedule for weekly government bond auctions to be held on Wednesdays in June hasn't been released yet. \par \par \uc2  The fragile government bond market wasn't ready to absorb any news about new supply, said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can restore stability in the market and recover some of the losses seen since Monday, however, next week's auction should proceed as usual, the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Money market interest rates fell from the highs set Wednesday and today on expectations that the incoming \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor will raise short-term interest rates it controls, due to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the repurchase market. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it injected THB20 billion worth of liquidity through repurchase market lending and bought between THB6 billion and THB8 billion worth of government bonds during Tuesday and Wednesday combined. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets continued Thursday. Repurchase market interest rates for terms of one-, two- and three-months came off highs or returned from periods of inactivity to close at last week's historic lows. \par \par \uc2  The overnight interbank rate closed around 2.25%-2.50%, down from a close Wednesday of 3.00%-3.25% and a high earlier that day at 3.75%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market       Term                       Thursday   Wednesday\par Interbank   Overnight/Most Active       2.5000%    2.2500%\par Repurchase  Overnight/Close             1.3125%    1.3750%\par             14-Day                      1.5000%    1.5000%\par             1-Month                     1.8125%  no activity\par             3-Month                     1.9375%  no activity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Supunnabul Suwannakij and Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662 266\par 0744; tom.fox@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx5v02g1e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
