\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Up Slightly, Shadowing Yen's Movements \b0\par\par\uc2 897 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2001\par 13:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht finished up slightly Tuesday, tracking the Japanese yen higher against the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 45.60 baht, down slightly from THB45.63 late Monday, but off an earlier low of THB45.52. \par \par \uc2  The yen's gains overnight pushed the U.S. unit down early, and local exporter dollar sales added to the pressure. \par \par \uc2  Volume was minimal, however. The dollar recovered slightly against the baht in the afternoon, shadowing its movements against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Offshore investors were sidelined ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's clarification on new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction reporting requirements to be implemented July 2. \par \par \uc2  "No one wants to take positions. Everyone is waiting for the meeting of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (with onshore bank treasury departments) June 1 to clarify how the new requirements will work," a dealer at a foreign bank said. \par \par \uc2  The baht is expected to trade in a narrow range until then, barring any regional volatility. The dollar is expected to open Wednesday between THB45.50 and THB45.70. \par \par \uc2  The reporting requirements will improve the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to monitor baht outflows and identify banks and clients involved in circumventing limits on those flows. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand limits the supply of baht to offshore \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap markets making it more expensive for speculators to short-sell the baht. \par \par \uc2  Restrictions on baht outflows and detailed reporting requirements have virtually eliminated speculative volumes, thereby limiting volatility and keeping the baht's depreciation in line with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The baht is expected to continue to weaken in the medium-term due to\par the dollar's bullish prospects against regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , domestic\par political uncertainty, economic weakness, and fragile global demand for\par exports.\par - - 22/05/01 11-18G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Government bond prices ended slightly lower, but confidence that the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and to keep interest rates low -\par despite government calls for a review of monetary policy - kept losses\par to a minimum in low volume.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yields of long-term government bonds rose two-to-three basis points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said Tuesday he had asked the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to review monetary policy and to report to the Cabinet next\par week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Several of Thaksin's advisers have criticized the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's low\par interest-rate policy, saying that it reduces public income from deposits\par and contributes to capital outflows, as companies are encouraged to\par refinance foreign debt at low domestic rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said the bond market was unaffected, as investors don't\par believe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul will alter the low\par interest rate policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  encourages local refinancing of foreign debt, and\par maintains low interest rates to support economic recovery and\par restructuring of corporate and public sector debt.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Monetary Policy Board will meet Thursday. It is\par expected to maintain the policy-signal 14-day repurchase market interest\par rate at 1.5%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market Tuesday to keep\par yields from rising amid the uncertainty. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought\par one-month and three-month Treasury bills, and three-year and 10-year\par government bonds, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The three-year bond closed at 2.80%, compared with a last deal of\par 2.85% Monday, while the 10-year finished at 5.20%, down from 5.202%. But\par the December 2008 issue closed at 4.68%, up from 4.65% Monday, the Thai\par Bond Dealing Center said in its daily report.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market was subdued ahead of an auction Wednesday of THB3.5 billion\par of nine-year government bonds and THB1.46 billion of a 20-year issue.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said the nine-year government bond traded Tuesday in a\par bid-offer range of 5.27% to 5.23%, around a last deal Monday of 5.2425%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The average accepted yield for the nine-year government bond is\par expected to be close to secondary market levels, between 5.22% and\par 5.30%, a dealer at a foreign bank said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The 20-year bond average accepted yield should be around 6.90% to\par 6.96%. Secondary market bids were around 6.96% Tuesday, but offers were\par scarce, the dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An auction Tuesday of government-guaranteed bonds issued by the state\par Financial Institutions Development Fund, or FIDF, resulted in premiums\par of just over 40 basis points on the government bond yield curve, as\par expected.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold THB2 billion of a two-year FIDF bond at a\par weighted-average of 3.012%, and THB2 billion of a five-year FIDF bond at\par an average of 4.1146%. The two-year bond closed in the secondary market\par at 3.00%, but the five-year issue was untraded.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investors began to show renewed interest in higher-yielding state\par enterprise and corporate bonds, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An issue by Advanced Info Service due March 2006 was traded at 4.76%,\par but closed at 4.78%, unchanged from Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Money market interest rates were mostly flat amid flush liquidity.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market       Term                       Tuesday   Monday\par Interbank   Overnight/Most Active       1.7500%   1.7500%\par Repurchase  Overnight/Close             1.2500%   1.3125%\par             14-Day                      1.5000%   1.5000%\par             1-Month                     1.8125%   1.8125%\par             3-Month                     1.9375%   1.9375%\par -By Laurent Malespine and Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744;\par tom.fox@dowjones.com\par -0- 22/05/01 11-31G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx5m02bc2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
