\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Slightly Up, Tracks Singapore Dlr Move Up \b0\par\par\uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2001\par 12:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht ended slightly higher against the U.S. dollar Wednesday, tracking the Singapore dollar's move up during the day, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 45.50 baht, down a touch from THB45.555 at the Asian close Friday. Thai markets were closed Monday and Tuesday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trade was relatively thin, with the U.S. dollar moving in a narrow range as most investors were sidelined in the absence of fresh, market moving news. \par \par \uc2  "It seems like most investors are still in holiday mood," said a dealer at a foreign bank. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht hit an early intraday low of THB45.62 against the U.S. dollar after the Singapore dollar touched its intraday low of $1.8250. \par \par \uc2  However, the baht later regained ground in line with the Singapore dollar's recovery due to fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Exporter dollar selling in the afternoon also helped support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to open in a range of THB45.40 and THB45.60 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said trade is likely to continue its slowing trend during the rest of the week as most investors await the U.S. Federal Reserves' Open Market Committee rate setting meeting May 15. \par \par \uc2  The FOMC is widely expected to further cut interest rates by 50 basis points to facilitate a soft-landing of the U.S. economy. Such expectations should give the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  a boost in the short-term, analysts said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Investors were also monitoring Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra's key\par speech at the Fortune Global Forum in Hong Kong late Wednesday. He is\par expected to give the right message to foreign investors in a bid to\par dispel thier fears created by his speech last month, suggesting Thailand\par may close its markets to foreigners.\par - - 09/05/01 10-36G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thai government bond prices ended down in sluggish trade, following a\par disappointing auction of nine-year government bonds earlier in the day,\par traders said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on the THB3 billion nine-year issue came at the higher end\par of expectations at 5.1475%, significantly up from 5.0828% at the\par previous auction.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The nine-year is seen as a trendsetter for the secondary market. Its\par higher yields in the auction triggered selling in other close tenors,"\par said a dealer at local bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the secondary market, the yield on the April 2010 issue closed at\par 5.17%, jumping from 5.07% at the previous close.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yields on most medium-term bonds also rose after the auction by\par five to six basis points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Among the most active issues, the bond maturing December 2006 closed\par at 3.95%, compared with 3.91% Friday. The July 2007 issue closed at\par 4.16%, compared with 4.10%, while the December 2008 issue finished at\par 4.60%, compared with 4.54%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thai markets were closed Monday and Tuesday for a religious holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A longer-term THB1.5 billion 20-year bond was sold at 6.9400% at the\par same Wednesday auction, however, unchanged from the last auction, with\par substantial demand from long-term investors such as insurance companies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will auction THB2 billion each of two-year and\par five-year government bonds, guaranteed by the state's Financial\par Institutions Development Fund, or FIDF, Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said despite strong interest for short-term instruments,\par yields on both FIDF issues may move up by 5 to 10 basis points, compared\par to the last auction, due to the result of the nine-year bond auction.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the previous auction, the two-year FIDF bond was sold at a\par weighted-average accepted yield of 2.8787%, while another five-year FIDF\par bond was sold at an average 3.9622%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Short-term money market rates were higher due to higher onshore\par implied interest rates and after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shifted its payment\par time for bond auctions to 0300 GMT from 0500 GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market       Term                      Wednesday     Friday\par Interbank   Overnight/Most Active      1.8750-2.00%  1.750%\par Repurchase  Overnight/Close            1.3125%       1.1875%\par             14-Day                     1.5000%       1.5000%\par             1-Month                    1.8750%       1.8750%\par             3-Month                    2.0000%       2.0000%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Suttinee Yuvejwattana, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744;\par suttinee.yuvejwattana@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx59021dm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
