\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Thailand \highlight\uc2  has no plan to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to lift baht-PM. \b0\par\par\uc2 132 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2001\par 07:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, April 18 (Reuters) - Thai Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said on Wednesday the government would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the baht, which usually follows the movement of other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We have to look at the whole region, we can't just \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make the baht unusally stronger than other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  He said he would rather say little about the baht and let the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monitor its movement. \par \par \uc2  "I understand the market mechanism of the baht and will not do anything that is against the nature (of the baht movement)," he said. \par \par \uc2  The baht has tumbled to three-year lows in recent weeks but has been stable this week at around 45.40 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4i01v9u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
