\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Finance Minister: No Need To Alter Interest Rates \b0\par\par\uc2 121 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2001\par 06:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR -(Dow Jones)- Thailand won't alter interest rates to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the volatility of the baht is a short-term problem, Thai Finance Minister Somkid Jatusripitak said Sunday, on the fringes of a finance ministers meeting of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations. \par \par \uc2  "No need," Somkid said in response to a question on changing interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "I believe in my country's fundamentals. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility is short-term, if we prepare in advance, nothing (negative) will happen," he added. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market last week to support the baht. \par \par \uc2  -By Tara Patel, Dow Jones Newswires; 603-2692-5254; tara.patel@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx4801kvw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
