\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST - Asian Wall Street Journal - April 6. \b0\par\par\uc2 370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2001\par 01:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, April 6 (Reuters) - The following stories were reported in Friday's electronic version of The Asian Wall Street Journal: \par \par \uc2  * Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose nearly two yen per dollar, regaining ground after recent steep declines. The increase happened after Tokyo leaders criticised "excessive" movements in the yen. \par \par \uc2  * Regional Wireless intends to bid on MobileOne, Singapore's number two cellular provider, possibly to counteract last month's unveiling of Singapore Telecommunicatikon bid for Cable & Wireless Optus . \par \par \uc2  * The United States and China held "intensive" talks on the release of the U.S. crew involved in a collision with a Chinese jet fighter. China repeated its demand for an apology, which Washington has ruled out. \par \par \uc2  * Indonesia adopted the IMF's new suggestions for its restructuring plans. But Texmaco's debt-workout accord seems to violate the new guidelines. \par \par \uc2  * Indian investigators blamed the pilots in the July 17 crash of an Alliance Air jet that killed 61, according to a report by the aviation ministry. \par \par \uc2  * Japan's ruling party set the date for a presidential election to choose a successor to Mori, after the prime minister declared his intent to resign. \par \par \uc2  * Mitsubishi Tokyo Financial said three of its banks expect to post combined net losses of $1.09 billion for the fiscal year, blaming large disposals of bad loans. \par \par \uc2  * Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Chatu Mongol confirmed the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Wednesday to slow - but not stop - the baht's weakening pace. The baht slid to a 37-month low against the dollar Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  * Taiwan President Chen Shui-bian met for the first time with the Dalai Lama in a long-anticipated session of friendly exchanges that underscored their common differences with China. \par \par \uc2  * Muslim rebels in the Philippines backed off a threat to behead a U.S. hostage in response to a last-minute appeal from his mother to let the captive go. \par \par \uc2  * Thaksin Shinawatra acknowledged that he could be found guilty of financial wrongdoing and forced to resign as Thailand's prime minister. \par \par \uc2  * Hitachi and Kawasaki Heavy teamed up in hopes that their railway expertise will help them win overseas orders as the Japanese market shrinks (From Nikkei Net Interactive). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4502lfw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
