\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Dn Late; Won,Taiwan Dlr Worst Hit \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2000\par 11:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  sagged late Thursday, with the South Korean won extending its recent inexorable decline as the market grew immune to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hackneyed verbal mediation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar also lost some ground, falling for the ninth consecutive session to a fresh 19-month low. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the Philippine peso and the Thai baht were weaker, while the Singapore dollar and the Indonesian rupiah were slightly firmer. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar will continue to be undermined by the cloudy outlook for the semiconductor sector, which their economies depend on, and easing demand from the U.S., which is slowing the pace of these countries' exports. \par \par \uc2  And as they compete for shrinking demand for their exports, both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also weaken in sync with the Japanese yen. The yen is widely expected to languish against the dollar, after Japan's political disarray drove it through the key Y110 support, analysts added. \par \par \uc2  "The three economies compete in exports to third markets," said Chi Lo, a senior economist at Standard Chartered Bank in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  Domestically, political uncertainties in Taipei and hiccups in South Korea's restructuring efforts will drive away foreign investors, who have already turned skittish toward emerging markets in recent months, said Lo. \par \par \uc2  "I do see further weakness in the short term for both the New Taiwan dollar and the Korean won," he added. "The domestic and external factors are turning against the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Lo said the U.S. dollar will likely strengthen further to NT$34 against the New Taiwan dollar and 1,200 won within the next three to six months, although the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be more volatile. \par \par \uc2  Leading the descent, the won lost 1.4% to hit its lowest level in more than a year due to local and offshore dollar demand, despite market rumors of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which subsequently appeared unfounded, and the trite verbal mediation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,193 won, its highest close since Nov. 2, 1999, when it ended at KRW1,193.30. The dollar closed Wednesday at KRW1,176.90. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were impervious to the all-too-familiar comments by the authorities about their concern over the recent fall in the won and their threat to adopt measures, if necessary, to slow down the decline. \par \par \uc2  "They're just giving lip service...there's no real action," said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Seoul. \par \par \uc2  "The only thing that will stop the won from weakening to KRW1,200, and beyond to KRW1,250, is the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming in aggressively to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but they're not doing it right now," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at a new 19-month high of NT$32.931, up from NT$32.870 Wednesday, as foreign equity funds spooked by domestic political uncertainties and a slowing economy flowed out Taipei. \par \par \uc2  Although the benchmark local stock index edged up 0.3% Thursday, foreign investors sold a net NT$1.40 billion on the Taiwan Stock Exchange, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Reversing from its recent gains, the Philippine peso retreated Thursday on expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut its policy rates as early as Friday, during its board meeting, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 49.380 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from Wednesday's close of PHP49.120. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it is too early to reduce overnight rates right now, but if stability in the peso continues, as the Philippine Senate begins the impeachment process against President Joseph Estrada over corruption allegations, a cut may happen sooner than later. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked overnight rates by four percentage points in mid-October to support the peso, which plunged to historic lows following allegations that President Joseph Estrada took millions of dollars in payoffs from illegal gambling syndicates and provincial tax revenues. Estrada has denied the charges. \par \par \uc2  After rebounding sharply in New York trading hours Wednesday, the Thai baht lost ground as tight liquidity in the forward market eased, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0915 GMT, the dollar was at 43.855 baht, up from the overnight low of THB43.35 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Higher offshore swap premiums, which raises short-term funding costs, had recently prompted offshore players to cover their short baht positions, fueling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains. \par \par \uc2  Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul said Thursday Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, but will do so as little as possible, implying that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely tolerate the baht's current levels. \par \par \uc2  Bucking the region's weakness, the Indonesian rupiah was slightly higher on dollar long-liquidation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to 9,410 rupiah from IDR9,450 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Thanks to the rupiah, the Singapore dollar was firmer at S$1.7548 against its U.S. counterpart, compared with S$1.7564 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwbn05okt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
