\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Up; Profit-Taking After Baht Support Holds \b0\par\par\uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2000\par 13:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht ended higher against the dollar Tuesday as investors took profits and unwound long-dollar positions after failing to breach resistance at 44.20 baht, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the baht, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 43.845 baht, down from THB43.99 late in offshore Asian trade Monday. Thai markets were closed for a public holiday Monday. \par \par \uc2  Offshore investors took advantage of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's absence Monday to push the dollar past THB44 for the first time since June 1998, but when a test of resistance failed at a high of THB44.17 Tuesday, long-dollar investors took profits and local exporters joined in the selling. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a foreign bank said the dollar is still very strong against global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  generally, but the current market exchange rate already reflects many of the negative factors weighing on the baht - from political uncertainty ahead of general elections expected between Dec. 16 and Jan. 6, to constraints on growth from high oil prices and slow progress in restructuring nonperforming loans. \par \par \uc2  The dollar should open Wednesday in a range of THB43.60 to THB44, but key support and resistance will be THB43.50 and THB44.20, respectively, he said. \par \par \uc2  Bandid Nijathaworn, Bank of Thailand's spokesman, said he was "concerned" by baht weakness, although it was due mainly to dollar strength. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was monitoring the market and it had noticed active volume offshore, especially in short-term transactions. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda and his deputy Pisit Leeahtam said separately Tuesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should continue to monitor the market and act to reduce speculative or unusual activity if possible. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they suspected rumors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was active in the market near the high were untrue. They believed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was purely verbal. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very unlikely to waste any of its reserves fighting the market, but it can - at relatively low cost - engineer short-term shortages of baht in offshore swap markets, which can hurt speculators by raising the costs of covering or maintaining long-dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  Government bond prices ended lower in light volume Tuesday, compared with closing prices Friday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A few foreign banks and funds did some panic selling early Tuesday when the market reopened after the Monday holiday, due to the baht's fall over the long weekend. \par \par \uc2  Buying by local banks in the afternoon helped bring prices off lows in thin volume. \par \par \uc2  The issue due 2006 closed at a yield of 4.57%, off a high of 4.60% but up from 4.52% last week. Yields move in the opposite direction to prices. \par \par \uc2  The 2008 issue closed at 5.43%, off a high of 5.45% but up from 5.36% Friday. The 2011 issue finished at 6.13%, off a high of 6.16% but up from 6.07%. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda said the government will issue THB20 billion worth of savings bonds to the public, of which THB10 billion will be issued this quarter. This is to help finance a planned budget deficit of THB105 billion in the year to Sept. 30, 2001. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said foreign banks won't be returning to buy in the bond market until they know more about the government's issuance plans, and about who will be running the finance ministry after elections expected in December or January. \par \par \uc2  They believe that at least THB50 billion worth of regular government bonds should be issued to help finance the deficit, in addition to the savings bonds. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry hasn't announced its plans yet, but another THB200 billion could be issued to cover losses of the state rescue fund, the Financial Institutions Development Fund, and another THB100 billion to refinance maturing issues. \par \par \uc2  The overnight interbank interest rate closed slightly higher as banks withheld funds at the beginning of a new biweekly reserve period, seeking higher yields. \par \par \uc2  Flush liquidity kept the implied tomorrow-next yield in the onshore swap market relatively flat at around 3%. \par \par \uc2  The overnight repurchase market rate closed down slightly from the Friday close, which was abnormally high due to demand from a foreign bank which was caught short late in the day when volume was very thin. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market     Term                  Tuesday        Friday\par Interbank  Overnight/Most active 2.5000%        1.7500%\par Repurchase Overnight/Close       1.6250%        1.8750%\par            14-Day                1.5000%        1.5000%\par            3-Month               2.4375%        2.4375%\par -By Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; tom.fox@dowjones.com\par -0- 24/10/00 11-33G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwao051xx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
