\par\par \b \uc2  Offshore players push Thai baht near key support. \b0\par\par\uc2 452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2000\par 07:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 23 (Reuters) - The Thai baht slid in thin offshore trade on Monday and dealers said the market appeared to be trying to test the key support level of 44.00 to the dollar, which has not been seen since June 1998. \par \par \uc2  Thai financial markets were closed for a holiday on Monday, limiting volume in the baht but also making offshore players bolder about pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. \par \par \uc2  "As the onshore markets are closed, offshore players are trying to speculate. They may try to push it beyond 44 some time later today," a dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted at 43.77/82 per dollar at 0451 GMT in Singapore against levels of around 43.48 late on Friday. It hit a low of 43.90 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said most offshore players had stayed on the sidelines on Friday after the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the market became too volatile. \par \par \uc2  But Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Assistant Governor Chettavee Charoenpitaks also said on Thursday he did not expect the baht to fall to 44 per dollar. Dealers said this had given the market a target. \par \par \uc2  Technically, the dollar is also expected to struggle near 44.00 as it has not firmed above that level since March 1998, when it was in a steep downtrend from an all-time high of 56.90 baht in January 1998. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments and stop-loss dollar orders linked to the expiry on Thursday of dollar/baht option positions helped keep some pressure off the baht on Friday. \par \par \uc2  But offshore players were spotted buying dollars at 43.60 baht before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments. \par \par \uc2  "Some of the offshore players were buying dollars at 43.60 which shows a lot of pent-up demand is there and probably that's what is happening today," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Mokhtar Hassan, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at IFR Asia Eye, said Thai authorities were unlikely to defend the baht at current levels, given the weakness of other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the yen, against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Technically the baht can fall to 44.40, but baht liquidity in the offshore forwards will be squeezed today because of the dollar buying and the baht should find some support due to the liquidity adjustment," Hassan said. \par \par \uc2  One-month offshore forwards were last quoted at -4.0/-2.5 satang/dollar against -6.0/-3.0 satang/dollar late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Investors have tended to short the baht as a proxy for less liquid Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Philippine peso and Indonesian rupiah as Thailand's low interest rates make it a prime funding vehicle. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwan03nve\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
