\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends At New Low; Dlr Likely To Break THB43 \b0\par\par\uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 13:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht fell to a fresh 27-month low against the dollar Friday as onshore corporate dollar buying persisted through rounds of offshore buying and profit-taking, and regional markets plunged on fears of a slowdown in global demand for computer chips, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished near the day's highs at 42.935 baht, up from THB42.67 at the Asian close Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar is likely to break through THB43.00 after the Bangkok close, but they expect THB43.20 to be much tougher to crack. \par \par \uc2  Resistance at THB43.20 should still be in place when the market reopens for the Asian day Monday, they said. \par \par \uc2  The Stock Exchange of Thailand Index of Thai stocks dropped 3.2% to 274.14 points, amid a regional selloff in countries with strong representation in the electronics sector. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said a warning of lower third-quarter gross margins by Intel Corp. (INTC) of the U.S. may be due to company-specific problems, rather than signifying a drop in global demand for chips. \par \par \uc2  But the gloom in equities only boosted the strength of the dollar's rise against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Almost all analysts expect the dollar to reach at least THB43.80, and possibly push past THB44 in the coming months, but many expect the baht to stage a recovery next year after the formation of a new government. Thai general elections are expected sometime between late November and January. \par \par \uc2  Foreign debt repayment by Thai state enterprises and corporates ahead of the end of the fiscal year and the Japanese half-year has stoked dollar demand, along with frantic hedging against further depreciation in the baht as the dollar threatens to continue climbing against all regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Thailand's fiscal year ends Sept. 30. \par \par \uc2  The government expects the situation to improve somewhat next month, when the pace of debt repayment eases, but investment inflows aren't expected to resume in strength until investors get a look at the new government. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul said Friday, however, that an agreement should be signed with the U.K.'s HSBC Holdings for the sale of the nationalized Bangkok Metropolitan Bank after he returns from abroad Oct. 6. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other officials have begun verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't expected to spend any foreign reserves unless the dollar reaches THB44, or begins to move too quickly on any given day. \par \par \uc2  Money market interest rates and implied yields in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap markets were flat or even eased slightly, amid flush liquidity both onshore and offshore. \par \par \uc2  The baht's weakness did cause offshore cross-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate swaps to firm slightly, however, and they are expected to rise about 10 basis points next week. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market     Term                     Friday    Thursday\par interbank  overnight/most active    2.00%     2.25\par repurchase overnight/close          1.2500%   1.3750%\par            14-day                   1.5000%   1.5000%\par            3-month                  2.4375%   2.4375%\par - - 22/09/00 11-08G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Government bond prices ended flat in very thin trade, as investors\par were hesitant to take positions ahead of the weekend before they could\par gauge the extent of baht weakness to be expected next week, dealers\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Banks and funds believe the dollar will peak between THB43 to THB44,\par which isn't far from current levels, and they are flush with liquidity,\par dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But they are unwilling to commit to the bond market due to the\par possibility of another rapid depreciation in the baht leading to another\par spike in swap yields, which attract funds away from the bond market and\par boost bond yields, they said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yields move in the opposite direction to prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on the six-year government bond finished flat at 4.825%. The\par eight-year issue was at 5.605%, from 5.60%, and the 11-year issue closed\par at 6.33%, from 6.335%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers estimated that cash trading volume in the over-the-counter\par market Friday was very thin, possibly less than THB3 billion, compared\par with averages in recent months of around THB5 billion to THB7 billion or\par more.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; tom.fox@dowjones.com\par -0- 22/09/00 11-13G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9m04nr8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
