\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Deputy PM:Further THB Weakness May Hit Trade Surplus \b0\par\par\uc2 167 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2000\par 10:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- Thai Deputy Premier Supachai Panitchpakdi said Friday that further weakness in the baht wouldn't help boost exports much, but it could reduce the trade surplus by increasing the cost of imported oil in baht terms. \par \par \uc2  Supachai, who is also commerce minister, told reporters that he preferred see a stable baht, but he didn't specify an acceptable level. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a policy of not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets against market forces, except to damp excess volatility or speculative activity. \par \par \uc2  The baht has weakened to around 40.84 baht to the dollar as of late Friday, from below THB38 in March. \par \par \uc2  Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry officials have stated repeatedly that they are unconcerned by the baht's recent weakness because it has been in line with movements in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  -By Thomas Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744; tom.fox@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw8p0457s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
