\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Up On Indonesian Rupiah Strength \b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2000\par 13:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht ended higher Thursday as the Indonesian rupiah's surge to a two-month high outweighed news of the conviction of a key Thai ruling party member on corruption charges, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "There is a very strong correlation between the baht and the rupiah, otherwise we could have seen pressure on the baht today," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah rose as Indonesian President Abdurrahman Wahid moved to appease the hostile Indonesian parliament by handing day-to-day \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Vice President Megawati Sukarnoputri. \par \par \uc2  News the constitutional court Thursday found former interior minister Sanan Kachornprasart, secretary-general of the ruling Democrat Party, guilty of concealing his wealth and barred him from office for five years hurt stock prices. Traders said the main reason the baht didn't fall was the rupiah's strength. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 40.755 baht compared with THB40.955 at the Asian close Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Sanan's conviction could prove harmful for the Democrats ahead of national elections by the end of the year. The conviction could push the dollar back above the key psychological barrier of THB41.00, they added. \par \par \uc2  However, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires Thursday he is comfortable with the baht's current trading range as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent weakness is helping high export growth rates. \par \par \uc2  Sonakul said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its low interest rate policy to boost growth and accelerate debt restructuring although he acknowledged the pace of bad loan reduction has been slow. \par \par \uc2  He dismissed recent media reports that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the baht from a 10-year low in late July but didn't rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the future, if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Short-term money market rates were flat to lower led by slightly lower onshore swap rates and flush liquidity, traders said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market     Term                    Thursday      Wednesday\par interbank  overnight/most active 1.5000%-1.7500%   1.7500%\par repurchase overnight/close          1.250%         1.1250%\par            14-day                   1.500%         1.5000%\par            3-month                  2.4375           n/a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Government bonds ended mostly higher Thursday, but fell back from\par highs in the afternoon when bidders refused to match offers, dealers\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yields ended about one-to-three basis points lower for most issues,\par according to the daily trading report of the Thai Bond Dealing Center.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volume was heaviest in the issue due 2004, on buying by investors who\par hold the issue maturing Aug. 31, who are looking for a replacement for\par the short end of their portfolio, a dealer at a foreign bank said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The LB046A due June 15, 2004, was last traded at a yield of 4.4975%,\par down from 4.535%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the long end of the curve, the yield on the 15-year issue rose to\par 6.785% from 6.77%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A dealer at another foreign bank said consideration of the possible\par long-term inflation effects of higher electricity and oil prices may\par slow the fall in yields of long-end bonds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, Bank of Thailand Governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul told Dow\par Jones Newswires Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is working on ways to\par upgrade and modernize the bond market and increase the effectiveness of\par monetary tools.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He expects a new payments system, which will guarantee delivery versus\par payment under the same system, will be in place in the third quarter of\par 2001 to accommodate bond trading volumes in the hundreds of billions,\par from current volumes usually less than THB10 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The new payments system will also enable the introduction of\par derivatives trading in the future, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A repurchase agreement market should also be launched next year in\par which Bank of Thailand will be a participant but not the operator, he\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Thomas Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662-266-0744;\par tom.fox@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw8a03wn9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
