\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Expected Lower Next Week \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2 766 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2000\par 10:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed late Friday, with the Thai baht and the Philippine peso resuming their recent downtrend, heralding more weakness for these Southeast Asian units. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had rebounded above the 41 baht psychological resistance level, to trade at 41.225 baht around 0815 GMT, up from THB40.925 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Against the peso, the dollar had risen to PHP44.980 earlier Friday, but retreated to PHP44.860 late in the day, little changed from Thursday's close of PHP44.865. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar continued its upward move after Thursday's strong gains, which were spurred by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's comments suggesting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be allowed to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was marginally firmer, although it remained stuck within its recent trading range. Despite heightened political uncertainty ahead of the country's National Assembly next month, investors are hesitant to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  having stepped up its vigilance to deter speculation against the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  A 4.8% plunge in the South Korean stock market stung the won, while month-end U.S. dollar-demand from local importers helped support the New Taiwan dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Political uncertainties in several Southeast Asian countries, the dollar's strength against the Japanese yen and strong local corporate demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to pressure Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  next week, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will still maintain their weak tone," said Thio Chin Loo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, market participants are awaiting the release of U.S. second-quarter gross domestic product data due later Friday. "Tonight's GDP will probably put to rest fears of a hard landing," said Thio. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht's rebound Thursday proved to be short-lived when the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the latest target for Southeast Asian bears - fell back below the THB41 support level Friday, on dollar-demand from local corporates and offshore dollar-buying, dealers said. The Thai baht, which had been under heavy pressure since the start of the week, was pushed to a 10-month low Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Amid political uncertainty ahead of Thailand's general elections later this year, many analysts expect the baht to weaken beyond THB42 to the dollar in the next few months. \par \par \uc2  In fact, a slide toward THB41.50 could come as early as next week, a dealer at an Asian bank said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai authorities' apparent tolerance for a weak baht had provided the catalyst for offshore investors to push down the baht, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Friday denied a report that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in London trading hours this week to defend the baht against speculative trade. \par \par \uc2  The baht's weakness is expected to continue dragging down the Philippine peso, which is already undermined by domestic political uncertainties and concerns over the government's yawning fiscal deficit. \par \par \uc2  In other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the movements of the rupiah and the Singapore dollar were tame. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 8,935 rupiah, down slightly from IDR8,963 late Thursday. Keen dollar demand around IDR8,900 will likely keep the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buoyed, while Indonesian state banks and offshore participants are expected to sell dollars around IDR9,150, a dealer at a U.K. bank said. \par \par \uc2  Against its Singapore counterpart, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at S$1.7322, down from S$1.7350. Participants are still smarting from the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unexpected announcement Thursday it was prepared to allow a modest appreciation of the Singapore dollar in relation to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its major trading partners. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, foreign fund equity outflows on the local stock market and dollar-buying in the non-deliverable forward market dented the South Korean won, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 1,116.60 won, up from Thursday's close of KRW1,113.40. \par \par \uc2  The won, which has been outperforming its other ailing Asian counterparts in recent months, could weaken further next week with the yen's weakness and as the stock market is pressured by concerns over the health of the country's conglomerates, said Thio at BNP Paribas. If the dollar breaks KRW1,120, it is set to strengthen to KRW1,130 thereafter, she added. \par \par \uc2  Against the New Taiwan dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at NT$31.050, barely changed from Thursday's close of NT$31.052. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7s03olb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
