\par\par \b \uc2  Baht shouldn't fall further - Thai deputy finmin. \b0\par\par\uc2 171 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2000\par 14:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, July 12 (Reuters) - Thai Deputy Finance Minister Pisit Leeahtam said on Wednesday the embattled baht should not weaken further as it was underpinned by improving fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "I can assure you that the baht should not weaken further because our economic fundamentals have strengthened over the past two years. Our foreign debt has declined a lot," Pisit told reporters before speaking at a seminar. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not put any pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite the baht's drop to nine-month lows. "We would not ask them to do anything against the market mechanism," he said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Wednesday, Deputy Prime Minister Supachai Panitchpakdi also said the baht should not fall further. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul said the current level of the baht was acceptable and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ((Bangkok newsroom +66-2 637-5500 ext 417 \par \par \uc2  email:bangkok.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw7c01p87\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
