\par\par \b \uc2  Thai baht recovers late, outlook negative. \b0\par\par\uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 12:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, May 12 (Reuters) - The Thai baht recovered from its lowest levels in five months on Friday on dollar profit-taking by offshore parties and onshore banks. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the outlook for the baht remained negative due to dollar strength against regional units amid an expectation of a possible rate hike in the U.S. on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The baht was traded at 38.83/38.86 per dollar late after having earlier hit an intraday low of 39.03. The Thai unit fell through support at 39 to hit a five-month low at 39.07 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the baht was at 35.0531/35.1009 late against 35.1524/35.2170 early. \par \par \uc2  "After the baht hit the day low of 39.03, offshore players unloaded the dollar to take profit from recent gains," said a dealer at a local bank. "They probably want to square positions ahead of the weekend." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said market was convinced the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the baht on Thursday by buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 39 to the dollar in the offshore market. \par \par \uc2  Tasana Rachatapo, director of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banking department, declined comment when asked whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  She said the weakness of the baht was due to on a halt in dollar sales by exporters amid persistent demand from oil firms. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said a decline in foreign reserves and forward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts last week could be connected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday foreign reserves stood at $31.8 billion on May 4 against $32.1 billion on April 28. It said it had net forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  contracts to buy Thai baht of $2.5 billion on May 4 against $2.6 billion on April 28. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not confirm or deny," said a dealer at a foreign bank. "It's obvious they did something yesterday but I do not see any such action today." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a hike in global oil prices and expectations of a 50 basis point increase in U.S. rates put more pressure on local oil companies to accelerate dollar buying in recent days. \par \par \uc2  They said the rise in the main Thai stock index on Friday after fours days of losses gave some psychological boost to the baht despite an uncertain outlook for the equity market. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the composite SET index closed up 2.71 percent at 345.67 due to a technical rebound, but it was down 9.9 percent on the week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said short-term offshore swap rates declined in late trade, reflecting easing demand for the dollar, after jumping almost 30 percent on an annualised basis due to recent weakness of the baht. \par \par \uc2  Tom/next dollar/baht offshore forwards were at a discount of -0.5/0.0 satang per dollar late against a premium of +5.0/+10.0 early. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/baht offshore forward premiums were unchanged at 10/13 satang per dollar late and onshore at a discount of -12.50/-11.75 satang. \par \par \uc2  The one-day interbank rate eased to 1.50-1.75 percent late against 1.75-2.00 percent early. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's one-day repurchase rate closed at 0.9375 percent, against 1.0000 percent in the morning. \par \par \uc2  ((Kettiya Jittapong, Bangkok Newsroom +66-2 637 5500 ext 418 