\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Thai  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2  to prop up baht. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kettiya Jittapong \par \uc2 638 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2000\par 09:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, May 11 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the baht on Thursday after the Thai unit broke down through support at 39 to the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The action pushed the baht back up through 39 and by 0720 GMT the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had recovered to 38.82/85 per dollar, but was still around five-month lows near a level last seen in early December. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have only a limited impact and several forecast the Thai unit could slip back again to test next support at 39.20 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The baht bounced back within 5-10 minutes after falling to the 39. It is obvious that there must be someone \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the offshore market...We assume it is the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a dealer at major Thai bank. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials were not immediately available for comment but three other dealers at major Thai banks confirmed they had seen the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the baht breached key support at 39 due to sustained dollar buying by offshore parties and corporates amid concerns over fund outflows and a possible U.S. rate hike. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a denial by the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance minister that financial authorities intended to impose capital controls gave psychological support to the Thai unit. \par \par \uc2  Thai finance minister Tarrin Nimmanahaeminda and the Bank of Thailand on Thursday denied rumours that they planned to impose capital controls to help prevent further falls of the baht. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  assistant governor Chetthavee Charoenpitak told reporters the baht weakness, which was in line with the movements of other regional units, was due to external factors and the selling of shares in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  FURTHER DECLINES SEEN \par \par \uc2  Chartists said the next target for dollar/baht was the 39.20 level, which marked a 50 percent Fibonacci retracement of the dollar's September 28 to January 3 decline on the daily chart. \par \par \uc2  "39.20 is actually the dollar resistance. It's not at this level (39.00)," said a baht trader. \par \par \uc2  A break beyond 39.20 would put the dollar on track for a further rise towards 39.80 baht, the 62.8 percent Fibonacci retracement of its September-January dollar decline. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they remained bearish on the Thai unit on expectations of persistent dollar demand from corporates to accelerate debt repayment ahead of a U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting on monetary policy on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve is widely expected to raise key U.S. interest rates by 50 basis points. \par \par \uc2  "It is a story of fund movements. Foreigners now are moving their money out of the Thai market as other global markets yield more attractive returns," said Anusorn Tamjai, macro economist at Citibank in Bangkok. \par \par \uc2  "There is no good reason for corporates to shoulder rising costs as a result of a widening gap in global interest rates. The hike in U.S. interest rates put pressure on Thai corporates to rush to repay foreign debts," he said. \par \par \uc2  Anusorn said he expected the baht to move in range of 39.00-39.50 in one-to-three month period. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said declines in the Thai equity market had dampened sentiment and dragged the baht lower as the market expected foreigners to continue to unload Thai shares and sell the baht. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's composite SET stock index closed down 1.67 percent at 337.81 at midday on Thursday, having fallen more than two percent at the opening. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors have sold a net of 23.14 billion baht worth of Thai shares so far this year against net sales of 3.13 billion for the whole 1999, stock exchange data show. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5b00p7a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
