\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Dep PM: Exports To Drive Economic Growth In 2000 \b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2000\par 09:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- Thai Deputy Prime Minister Supachai Panitchpakdi said Monday that exports will remain a major driver of economic growth in 2000, but that public sector spending will cease to be much of a factor after September. \par \par \uc2  Supachai told a news conference after a meeting of economic ministers that it will be difficult to continue deficit fiscal spending to stimulate the economy once the current fiscal year has ended on Sept. 30, because it would cause too much of an increase in public sector debt. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's public sector debt rose to 36.8% of GDP as of the end of September 1999, from 15.3% three years earlier. \par \par \uc2  The Government Spokesman's Office said in a statement issued at the news conference that the country's total foreign debt dropped to $74.6 billion at the end of 1999 from $86.2 billion the previous year, however, as the private sector continued to pay down foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  Supachai said the Export-Import Bank of Thailand will continue to increase the availability of packing credits to exporters, and the government will help the private sector accelerate debt restructuring to encourage investment. \par \par \uc2  Economic ministers received a report Monday on previously-released gross domestic product growth estimates for 1999 and 2000, of 4.0% for both years from Bank of Thailand, and of 4.1% and 4.4%, respectively, from a state planning agency. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said exports grew 7% in U.S. dollar terms in 1999, and are forecast to grow 7.4% this year. \par \par \uc2  Supachai said economic ministers were informed that the country's trade surplus could fall to around between $3 billion and $4 billion in 2000, from around $8.5 billion last year and $10.2 billion in 1998, and that a trade deficit is possible in 2001. \par \par \uc2  If the trade balance is still in deficit in 2002, and public sector debt remains high, it could have a negative effect on the economy, he said. \par \par \uc2  Therefore, Bank of Thailand will keep a close eye on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, because if the baht gets too strong it could hurt the country's exports, he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the baht \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from getting too strong, but it hasn't distorted the market, Supachai said. \par \par \uc2  He gave no details, nor did he mention in what time period the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a policy to buy dollars on weakness to boost foreign reserves and to prevent any excessive volatility. \par \par \uc2  Foreign reserves rose $800 million between Dec. 24 to Dec. 30, 1999, of which $500 million was accounted for by transactions with local financial institutions in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap markets, to boost baht liquidity in case of excess foreign demand over the New Year. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand may have purchased up to $300 million dollars in the spot market over the period, but it normally doesn't comment on its market transactions. \par \par \uc2  Supachai, who is also commerce minister and is responsible for export promotion, has consistently expressed more concern about the possible negative effects of a strong baht than Finance Ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials. \par \par \uc